                <pb facs="0221" n="123" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg123"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/1146: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-05-24T16:00:00+08:00">May 24, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 24—9:10 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>367. Legation’s 365, May 23, 6 p.m., paragraph 2.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d100-f1"
                    /> Following telegram dated April 23 has been received from the Commander in
                    Chief, United States Asiatic Fleet: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The American Minister has written to the Consul General at Hankow and
                            advised him that Americans should be discouraged from going to Kuling
                            this summer. I have discussed the question with the Minister and
                            informed him that it would be impracticable to afford protection with
                            naval forces.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
